From 82906c1efacb1262fad19b94f5dc727bb5209921 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Rajan <7121943+kvnloo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 02:31:20 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(worktree): don't track the base when it is on a remote
 not named origin

"New worktree from base" passes --no-track so a branch made from a
remote-tracking ref is standalone, not wired to that ref as its
upstream. The flag was only added when the base started with "origin/".
The base picker also lists refs from other remotes. In a fork layout
(origin = your fork, upstream = the project), branching from
upstream/main made the new branch track upstream/main. Review push then
calls a bare git push, which push.default=simple rejects because the
branch name doesn't match its upstream.

Pass --no-track for every base, in both twins (Electron
git-worktree-ops.ts and the hermes_cli/web_git.py mirror). Under git's
default branch.autoSetupMerge a local base is not tracked anyway; under
always/inherit --no-track keeps that branch standalone too. The
origin-only best-effort fetch is unchanged.

The existing origin/main no-tracking test in each twin now also runs
with the remote named upstream.

Co-authored-by: detail-app[bot] <180357370+detail-app[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
